===== path resume
Resume an agent session into the chosen harness, projecting the document and exec'ing the harness's resume command

Usage: path resume [OPTIONS] [INPUT]

Arguments:
  [INPUT]
          Toolpath document to resume from. Accepted shapes: a Pathbase URL (`https://host/owner/repo/slug`), a bare Pathbase shorthand (`owner/repo/slug`), a path to a local toolpath JSON file, or a cache id (e.g. `claude-abc`, `pathbase-foo-bar-baz`). With no input, a terminal UI lists the sessions in the document cache

Options:
  -C, --cwd <CWD>
          Working directory to run the resumed harness from. Defaults to the current shell cwd. The on-disk projection is keyed on this directory and the harness will be exec'd with cwd set to it

      --harness <HARNESS>
          Pin the resume target. Skips the interactive picker
          
          [possible values: claude, gemini, codex, opencode, cursor, cursor-agent, pi, copilot]

      --no-cache
          Skip the cache entirely when fetching from Pathbase: don't read an existing entry, don't write the fetched body. Useful for ephemeral environments where you don't want the cache to grow

      --force
          Force a re-fetch from Pathbase even if a cache entry exists, overwriting it with the new bytes. Default behavior is to use the cached doc on hit and never round-trip

      --url <URL>
          Pathbase server URL. Falls back to the stored session's URL, then `$PATHBASE_URL`, then `https://pathbase.dev`

      --pretty
          Pretty-print JSON output

      --picker <PICKER>
          Backend for the interactive fuzzy picker used by `share`, `resume`, and `p import <provider>`. `auto` (default) uses the embedded skim picker and falls back to external `fzf` only when skim isn't compiled in; a hint is printed if `fzf` is also on PATH. `fzf`/`skim` force one backend and error if it isn't available

          Possible values:
          - auto: Pick whichever backend is available; prefer the embedded skim picker and fall back to external `fzf` when skim isn't compiled in. When external `fzf` is also on `PATH`, a one-time hint is printed to stderr advising `--picker fzf` for users who want their fzf config / keybindings
          - fzf:  Force the external `fzf` binary. Errors if it isn't on `PATH`
          - skim: Force the embedded skim picker. Errors if path-cli was built with `--no-default-features`
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
